course: medicine
lesson: 35
title: Screens, eyes and sleep
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Screens are not poison for your eyes, but when you stare at a game you blink much less, so your eyes dry out and feel tired; looking far away and blinking for a moment fixes it.
explain: The real eye worry for today's children is short sight, which is becoming much more common, and studies suggest that time outdoors in daylight, a couple of hours a day, lowers the risk.
explain: Sleep is when your body grows, your brain files away what you learned and your mood resets: children of 6 to 12 need about 9 to 12 hours a night.
explain: A phone in bed steals that sleep: the exciting game, the last video, the bright light, and before you know it it's midnight and tomorrow's maths feels like fog.
fact: Long screen time can mean less sleep, less activity and more tired eyes, though screens aren't always bad and balance matters.
fact: Staring at screens makes you blink less, so eyes get dry and tired, but screens don't permanently damage the eyes.
fact: The 20-20-20 rule says every 20 minutes look at something 20 feet (about 6 metres) away for 20 seconds.
fact: Sitting close to the TV may tire the eyes but doesn't damage them or cause short sight; a child who always sits close should have an eye test.
fact: Reading in dim light can make eyes tired but doesn't ruin eyesight.
fact: Research suggests time outdoors in daylight lowers the risk of children becoming short-sighted.
fact: Screens before bed, with bright light and exciting content, can delay sleep, so doctors suggest no screens for about an hour before bed and devices charged outside the bedroom.
fact: Night mode on a phone has not been shown to fix the sleep problem on its own.
fact: Children aged 6 to 12 need about 9 to 12 hours of sleep a night to grow, learn and stay healthy.
fact: Too little sleep affects mood, memory, concentration and growth, and catching up at weekends doesn't fully fix it; sleeping in very late can also upset the body clock, the body's sense of when to sleep and wake.
fact: Nightmares are common in children, are often linked to stress or scary stories, and don't predict the future.
fact: Sleepwalking is common in children, happens in deep sleep and is usually harmless; a sleepwalker should be guided gently back to bed, not shouted at.
exercise: Why do eyes feel tired after a long game? | You blink less, so they dry out | Screens slowly burn the back of the eye | Games use up your eye muscles for ever | The light makes eyes smaller
exercise: Do screens permanently damage the eyes? | No | Yes, after about an hour of looking | Yes, if they're bright | Only phones do, because they're so close
exercise: What is the 20-20-20 rule? | Every 20 minutes, look 20 feet away for 20 seconds | 20 games, 20 snacks, 20 minutes sleep | Sit exactly 20 cm from the screen, every 20 minutes, for 20 games | Blink 20 times every 20 hours
exercise: Does sitting close to the TV ruin your eyes? | No, but always sitting close may mean an eye test is needed | Yes, it causes short sight for life | Yes, permanently, after a few years | Only cartoons do, because they're so bright and fast
exercise: Does reading in dim light ruin your eyes? | No, it can only make them tired | Yes, it slowly causes blindness | Yes, by the age of ten | Only with small print in old books
exercise: What may lower the risk of short sight? | Time outdoors in daylight | Eating more sweets | Sitting much closer to screens | Wearing sunglasses indoors all day
exercise: How many hours of sleep do children of 6 to 12 need? | About 9 to 12 | About 5 to 6 | About 14 to 16 | About 7 at most
exercise: Why can't you sleep after using your phone in bed? | Bright light and exciting content delay sleep | Phones make the bed too hot to sleep in | Phones drain your energy along with their battery | You are not tired enough to sleep ever
exercise: What do doctors suggest about screens and bedtime? | No screens for about an hour before bed | Screens until you fall asleep, then switch off | Only night mode is needed, nothing else | Phones kept under the pillow, on silent
exercise: Does night mode fix the sleep problem? | Not on its own | Yes, it fixes it completely | Yes, and it even adds more sleep | It makes you sleep 12 hours
exercise: Can lost sleep be fully caught up at weekends? | No, not fully | Yes, every single bit of it | Yes, if you sleep double the hours | Only on Sundays, not Saturdays
exercise: Do nightmares predict the future? | No | Yes, sometimes | Yes, always | Only on birthdays
exercise: When are nightmares more likely? | With stress or scary stories | After a calm and quiet evening at home | Never in children, only in grown-ups | Only in summer, when it's hot at night
exercise: Is sleepwalking dangerous for most children? | Usually harmless | Always dangerous, wake them at once | It means there is a disease of the brain | It only happens to adults
exercise: What should you do if your brother sleepwalks? | Guide him gently back to bed | Shout loudly in his ear to wake him | Splash cold water on his face | Lock him outside
exercise: Can lots of screen time take away from other things? | Yes, less sleep and less activity | No, never, screens are extra time | Only for grown-ups who work on them | It actually adds more sleep at night
test: Gran: "sit back from the telly or you'll go blind!" What's true? | It won't damage eyes, but if he always sits close, check his sight | She's right, it causes blindness | It makes the eyes stronger, like exercise does for muscles | It only matters for old colour TV sets
test: Mate games till midnight, says he's "fine". What's the catch? | Feeling fine doesn't mean enough sleep; mood and memory suffer | He's fine, kids only need about 6 hours a night | Gaming rests the brain as well as sleep does | Midnight is a normal bedtime at his age
test: Two classes: one plays outdoors two hours a day, one stays in. Which is likely to have less short sight years later? | The outdoor class | The indoor class | Both the same, certainly | Neither will need glasses
test: "I'll just put night mode on and scroll in bed till 11" — will that fix sleep? | Not on its own, the content and late hour still steal sleep | Yes, perfectly | Yes, it adds extra hours of deep sleep | Night mode makes you sleep at once
test: What would happen if a 9-year-old slept only 6 hours every night for a month? | Mood, concentration and memory would likely suffer | Nothing, 6 hours is plenty for a child | She'd grow faster, with more time awake | She'd get cleverer from all the extra hours awake reading
test: Your eyes sting after 2 hours on a tablet. Quick fix? | Look far away and blink for a bit | Rub them hard with your knuckles | Get closer to the screen so they focus | Turn the brightness up to full
test: Little sis dreamt Mum disappeared and she's sobbing. What to tell her? | It was a nightmare, dreams don't predict the future | It might come true, so watch Mum carefully | Dreams are messages from the future | Don't tell anyone about it, ever
test: Brother's sleepwalking towards the stairs. What do you do? | Gently guide him back to bed and tell a parent | Scream to wake him | Push him back towards his room | Ignore it, sleepwalkers always stop safely at the stairs
test: Where's best to charge your phone at night? | Outside your bedroom | Under your pillow, so you hear it | On the bed next to your face | In the bathroom
test: In a movie, a kid who reads by torchlight under the covers goes blind. Real life? | Dim light may tire eyes but doesn't ruin them | It does cause blindness after a while | It's good for eyes, it trains them in the dark | Torches are banned in bedrooms
test: Sleep in the weekend till 1pm to "catch up" — does it work? | Not fully, and it can mess up your body clock | Yes, perfectly | Yes, it gives extra energy all week | It replaces the weekday sleep exactly, hour for hour
exam: Pursuant to current recommendations, determine the nightly duration of sleep required by children aged between six and twelve years. | Approximately nine to twelve hours | Approximately five to six hours | Approximately fourteen to sixteen hours | Approximately three hours
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning prolonged viewing of a television at close range. | It may induce fatigue but does not damage the eyes | It induces short sight permanently in the majority of children | It induces blindness in later life | It strengthens the eyesight
exam: Identify the activity which research suggests diminishes the risk of short sight developing in childhood. | Time spent outdoors in daylight | Viewing screens at close range | Reading in dim light | Wearing tinted spectacles indoors
exam: Whereas a child is observed sleepwalking, ascertain the conduct customarily advised. | Gentle guidance of the child back to bed | Abrupt awakening by shouting | Confinement in a locked room | Application of cold water
